 "schema": "osap-social-relevance-rules/1",
 "mode": "on",
 "note": "Extra rules for social media posts, on top of the news policy (tools/relevance.json). tools/social_relevance.mjs reads both on every refresh; changing a word list here changes what the Social media view shows, with no new code. Social posts are TV and radio desks' video titles more than headlines, so the news lists miss a lot both ways: a budget or casino-money story has no security word, and a football match on a news channel's live stream has no sport word. A post is checked in this order: Drop words are checked on the title only (a video's description often holds an incidental 'highlights' or 'podcast'); the other lists on the title and description. The original of a translated post is checked only against the 'native' words for its language (Spanish 'mayor', Italian 'un' and Dutch 'VS' are not the English words). 1. any news 'strong' word, or a social 'strong' word, keeps it; 2. any news 'drop' word, or a social 'drop' word, leaves it out; 3. any news 'keep' word, a social 'keep' word, or a 'native' word in the post's own language keeps it; 4. a 'bulletin' word in the title keeps it (a whole news programme: 'Evening News', 'Dnevnik', 'JT 20H'); 5. a post the translation step left in its own script is kept, unchecked; 6. anything else is left out. Words match as in tools/relevance.json (start of a word, any case and accent; a word ending in $ must end there, plural allowed). Measured against the 400-post hand check, tools/eval/social-relevance-sample.json: node tools/relevance_sample.mjs eval. \"mode\": \"on\" marks left-out posts; \"shadow\" only reports. Left-out posts are not deleted: the Social media view keeps them behind 'Show left-out posts'.",
